/**
 * Pest library — striped bark scorpion, Centruroides vittatus.
 *
 * All facts below verified Aug 2026 against Texas A&M AgriLife Extension:
 *  texasinsects.tamu.edu/striped-bark-scorpion; AgriLife E-362 "Scorpions";
 *  mytexas.ag.tamu.edu "Scorpions: Identification, Habitat and Control in
 *  Texas"; texasinsects.tamu.edu/scorpionida; "Venomous Terrestrial Animals of
 *  Texas"; AgriLife Today 30 Jun 2018 and 11 Sep 2023 (Molly Keck).
 *
 * ID: adults ~2 3/8 in (60 mm), up to ~2.5 in. Yellowish-tan, TWO broad
 *   blackish longitudinal stripes on the upper abdomen, dark triangular mark on
 *   the head over the eyes. Slender pedipalps, long slender tail; males longer
 *   tails. Juveniles lighter with dark pedipalp bases and dark terminal tail
 *   segment. Fluoresces under UV.
 * Life cycle: mate fall/spring/early summer; gestation ~8 MONTHS; LIVE BIRTH of
 *   13-47 young, average 31; young ride the mother's back 3-7 days through a
 *   molt; 5-6 molts to maturity; E-362 gives lifespan ~4 years (Field Guide
 *   says 3-8, some sources 20-25 — cite 4 and note variation).
 * Texas has ~18 species. C. vittatus occurs throughout the state and is the
 *   only species in the eastern half. Diversity rises westward: AgriLife counts
 *   4 near Amarillo, 3 near Abilene, 5 near Fort Stockton, 8 near Fort Davis,
 *   14 in Big Bend.
 *
 * ⚠️ NOT FOUND — do not write: a named species list for our ten counties.
 *   AgriLife gives counts for Amarillo/Abilene/Fort Stockton but NOT for
 *   Lubbock or Midland-Odessa. Do NOT name additional Permian Basin species.
 *
 * SEVERITY — must stay accurate and non-alarmist. AgriLife: "The stings from
 *   Texas scorpions produce only moderate reactions in most people because the
 *   poison has little effect on the nervous system." And: "None of the
 *   scorpions in Texas are considered 'deadly.'" The dangerous US species is
 *   the ARIZONA bark scorpion, C. sculpturatus — a different animal.
 *   No page on this site may imply a Texas species is lethal.
 */
