---

<BaseLayout
  title="Striped Bark Scorpion | West Texas Pest Library"
  description="Identification, biology, why they come indoors and what actually works, sourced to Texas A&amp;M AgriLife. The defining household pest of the Permian Basin."
  path="/pest-library/striped-bark-scorpion/"
  quickAnswer={quickAnswer}
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <Crumbs items={crumbs} />

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Pest library · <em>Centruroides vittatus</em></p>
      <h1>Striped bark scorpion</h1>
      <div class="measure"><AnswerBox text={quickAnswer} /></div>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          This is the defining household pest of the Permian Basin and the southern Llano Estacado. More
          summer calls come in about scorpions than about everything else combined, and more bad
          information circulates about them than about any other pest we deal with.
        </p>
        <p>
          So this page is sourced. Everything below comes from Texas A&amp;M AgriLife Extension
          publications, and where AgriLife and popular belief disagree, we have said so and gone with
          AgriLife.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Start here: they are not deadly</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          AgriLife's position could not be plainer. <strong>"None of the scorpions in Texas are considered
          'deadly.'"</strong> And: <strong>"The stings from Texas scorpions produce only moderate
          reactions in most people because the poison has little effect on the nervous system."</strong>
        </p>
        <p>
          The species people are actually thinking of when they worry about this is the
          <strong>Arizona bark scorpion</strong>, <em>Centruroides sculpturatus</em>, which is a genuinely
          medically significant animal and a different one. It is not the scorpion in your garage in
          Seminole.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>What a sting is actually like.</strong> Sharp, immediate pain. Localised swelling that
          often persists for days. Itching. Individual reaction varies considerably, and as with any
          arthropod venom an allergic reaction is possible. An ice pack handles the pain for most people.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>When to take it further.</strong> If pain or swelling persists, if there is any
          difficulty breathing, or if the person stung is a young child or has a known allergy. That is a
          medical decision and it belongs to a doctor or to poison control, not to us.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Why we lead with this.</strong> Because fear is the standard sales lever in this trade
          and we would rather not use it. Scorpions are a real nuisance, they are genuinely unpleasant to
          find in a bed or a boot, and there are good reasons to deal with them. Lethality is not one of
          them, and a company that implies otherwise is contradicting the state's own extension service.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Identification</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          <strong>Size.</strong> Adults around two and three-eighths inches, occasionally up to about two
          and a half.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Colour and markings.</strong> Yellowish-tan overall, with <strong>two broad blackish
          stripes running lengthwise down the upper abdomen</strong> — the feature the common name refers
          to — and a <strong>dark triangular mark on the head, over the eyes</strong>. That triangle is the
          detail most people miss and it is diagnostic.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Shape.</strong> Slender pedipalps — the pincers — rather than heavy crab-like ones, and a
          long slender tail. Males have longer tails than females.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Juveniles</strong> are lighter overall, with dark brown or black bases to the pedipalps
          and a dark terminal tail segment.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Under ultraviolet light the whole animal fluoresces</strong>, which is genuinely useful
          rather than a curiosity — see the survey section below.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>What it gets confused with.</strong> Pseudoscorpions, which are tiny and have no tail at
          all, and are harmless. Further west in Texas there are other species — AgriLife's El Paso office
          documents the Arizona stripetail, <em>Paravaejovis spinigerus</em> — but that is a far-west
          Texas record rather than a Permian Basin one.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>How many species are there?</strong> Texas has around eighteen. <em>C. vittatus</em>
          occurs throughout the state and is the only species in the eastern half, with diversity rising as
          you go west: AgriLife gives counts of four species near Amarillo, three near Abilene, five near
          Fort Stockton, eight near Fort Davis and fourteen in Big Bend. We are not going to name
          additional species for Gaines County or the Midland–Odessa area, because AgriLife does not
          publish a species list for those counties and we are not going to invent one.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Biology, and why it explains the pattern you are seeing</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          <strong>They give live birth.</strong> Not eggs. Mating occurs in autumn, spring or early
          summer, gestation runs around <strong>eight months</strong>, and the female produces between
          thirteen and forty-seven live young, averaging about thirty-one.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The young ride on her back</strong> for three to seven days, through their first molt,
          before dispersing. If you have ever turned over a board and found what looked like one large
          scorpion wearing a pale crust, that is what you were looking at.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Five or six molts to maturity</strong>, and AgriLife's E-362 gives a lifespan of around
          four years. Other sources range higher — the Field Guide says three to eight — so treat four as
          the working figure and expect variation.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Why this matters practically.</strong> An eight-month gestation and a four-year lifespan
          mean a property with an established population has overlapping generations rather than a
          seasonal flush. That is why a single heavy treatment does not clear a property permanently, and
          why the honest recommendation on a badly affected property is a programme rather than a visit.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>They are nocturnal, and that is thermoregulation rather than preference.</strong>
          AgriLife notes that hiding by day helps them manage temperature and water balance — functions
          that matter enormously for survival in dry habitat. Everything about their behaviour follows from
          the need not to dry out.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>And they climb well.</strong> AgriLife records that they "often are found in the attics
          of homes." People consistently underestimate this and search at floor level.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Why they come indoors here</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          This is the part that makes scorpions a regional story rather than a generic one, and the
          extension sources are unusually direct about it.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>AgriLife's E-362:</strong> "During periods of hot weather, scorpions may move into living
          areas to escape the high temperatures in attics."
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Molly Keck, AgriLife Extension entomologist:</strong> during hot, dry weather "they may
          seek out water and/or a more hospitable environment." And, during the 2023 drought, that
          scorpions pursue "water and cooler temperatures and when they feel that temperature difference
          below a door or by another small crack, they can squeeze their way inside."
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Put those together and you get the actual sequence</strong>, which explains almost every
          call we take:
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>They enter low.</strong> Under a door with a worn sweep, through a gap at a threshold,
          around a utility penetration — anywhere they can feel a temperature or humidity gradient. Their
          flattened body, as Wizzie Brown of AgriLife notes, lets them get into small cracks and under
          stones, bark and wood.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>They live high.</strong> Wall voids and attics, where it is dark and undisturbed.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Then the attic overheats</strong>, in the worst weeks of a Permian Basin summer, and they
          come down into the living space. Which is why people find them in July and August, in bedrooms
          and bathrooms, having seen nothing all spring.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The predictive value of this is real.</strong> A long dry stretch in June, with no rain
          and sustained heat, is the most reliable forecast available for a difficult July. If you have had
          six weeks of dry heat and you are on a property with history, that is the moment to act rather
          than the moment to wait and see.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>And it explains the moisture paradox.</strong> In a region this dry, any reliable water
          on a property — an irrigation head against a foundation, a condensate line, a leaking spigot, a
          swamp cooler overflow — is a destination. Fixing it does more for a scorpion problem than most
          treatments will, and it is free.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Surveying with ultraviolet light</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          Because the cuticle fluoresces, a UV torch turns an invisible problem into a visible one, and it
          is the single most useful thing a homeowner in this region can buy.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Go out after full dark</strong>, on a warm night, when they are active. Cool nights and
          daylight both produce misleadingly clean results.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Walk the perimeter of the house first</strong>, slowly, sweeping the beam along the
          foundation line, the base of walls, around door thresholds and up the lower part of the
          structure. Then the obvious harbourage: woodpiles, stacked material, landscape timbers, rock
          features, the edges of beds, anything sitting on the ground.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Then look up.</strong> Fence tops, the lower courses of brick, tree trunks, and the wall
          up toward the roofline. They climb, and a survey conducted entirely at ankle height understates
          what is there.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Then inside</strong>, if you are willing: the garage, the utility room, the corners of
          rooms where they meet the skirting, and — carefully, and only if it is safe to be up there — the
          attic.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>What the survey is for.</strong> Not for killing what you find, though you may. It is for
          learning <em>where they are</em>, which is the information that determines everything about how
          the property should be treated. A survey that shows heavy activity along one fence line and
          nothing elsewhere describes a completely different job from one showing scattered activity all
          round.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you do this before we come out, tell us what you found and where. It is genuinely more useful
          than anything else you can give us, and it costs the price of a torch.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>What actually works</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          AgriLife's management guidance leads with non-chemical measures, and so do we, because on this
          particular pest the ranking is not close.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Remove the harbourage.</strong> Trash, logs, boards, stones and bricks around the
          structure. This is the highest-value action available and it is free. Everything stacked against
          or near a wall is habitat, and scorpions are found under exactly these things.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Mow short near the foundation</strong>, and prune shrubs and overhanging branches back
          from the building.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Keep garbage above ground level.</strong>
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Never bring firewood indoors</strong> unless it is going straight onto the fire.
          AgriLife is explicit about this and it is one of the most common ways a scorpion gets into a
          living room.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Weather-strip loose doors and windows.</strong> The gap under an exterior door is the
          principal entry on most properties, and a worn sweep on a house past twenty years old in this
          climate is close to universal.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Plug weep holes</strong> — AgriLife suggests steel wool, copper mesh, a nylon scouring
          pad or squares of screen wire. Weep holes are a designed drainage feature and should not be
          sealed shut, but they should be screened, because they open straight into the wall cavity.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Seal roof eaves and pipe penetrations</strong>, and keep window screens in repair.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Where treatment fits.</strong> After all of that, not instead of it. A residual
          application aimed at the routes between harbourage and structure, at entry points, and at the
          places the UV survey showed activity does real work. The same product broadcast across a lawn
          does very little, because that is not where they are.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>What we will tell you honestly.</strong> On a property backing onto open ground, rock or
          heavy landscaping, the source is not going anywhere and elimination is not the goal. Suppression
          plus exclusion is, and it works. Anyone promising you a scorpion-free property is describing
          something they cannot deliver.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>The scorpion year in West Texas</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          <strong>Winter.</strong> Not gone — sheltered. Scorpions overwinter in protected harbourage,
          including inside structures, and a warm spell can produce a sighting in February that people find
          baffling. This is the best time of year to do the exclusion work, because you can see the ground
          and nobody is trying to do it in a hundred degrees.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Spring.</strong> Activity resumes as nights warm. Mating occurs in this window as well as
          in autumn. The first calls of the year usually come in April or May and they are the useful ones,
          because acting then is acting ahead of the problem.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Early summer.</strong> Activity climbing steadily. This is the window where a UV survey
          is most informative, because there is enough movement to map and not yet enough pressure to panic
          about.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>High summer — July and August.</strong> Peak. Attics reach their maximum temperature,
          the ground outside is at its driest, and both drivers AgriLife identifies are pushing the same
          direction at once. This is when scorpions come down out of wall voids into living space, and it
          is when the calendar genuinely fills.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>After a rain event.</strong> Worth watching. A heavy rain floods harbourage and pushes
          activity around in ways that can produce a sudden cluster of sightings on a property that had
          been quiet.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Autumn.</strong> Declining, but this is also a mating period, and cooling nights give
          another reason to move toward the warmth of a structure. The exclusion work done now pays
          through the winter.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Living with them sensibly</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          A few habits worth having in this part of Texas, none of which cost anything.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Shake out boots and shoes</strong> left in a garage, a mudroom or by a door. This is the
          single most common sting scenario out here.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Check bedding and towels</strong> in a property with known activity, particularly in
          July and August.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Do not put hands into places you cannot see</strong> — under stacked material, behind
          stored boxes in an attic, into a woodpile. Gloves for that work.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Watch the doorway at night.</strong> They come to lit thresholds because the light
          attracts the insects they eat. A porch light on all night beside a door with a worn sweep is an
          arrangement working against you.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Deal with the insects.</strong> Scorpions are predators and a property with heavy insect
          pressure supports more of them. General pest control on the perimeter reduces the food supply,
          which is an indirect but genuine part of the answer.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>And keep the moisture off the building.</strong> The irrigation head, the condensate
          line, the dripping spigot. In this climate that fix does more than it would anywhere wetter.
        </p>
        <p style="color:var(--ink-3);font-size:.95rem">
          Biology, severity and management guidance on this page follow Texas A&amp;M AgriLife Extension
          publications, including E-362 and the Texas Insects field guide. Medical questions about a sting
          belong to a doctor or to poison control rather than to a pest control company.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Faq items={faqs} heading="Scorpion questions" />
  <Cta
    line="Walk the perimeter with a UV torch after dark and tell him what lit up and where. That map is worth more than anything else you can describe over the phone."
  />
</BaseLayout>
